#
# ⚑⚑⚑ THIS GATE REFUSES WHEN IT CANNOT RUN. Every tool it needs is checked for FIRST, and an
# absent one is a refused commit rather than a skipped check. The failure this forbids is
# measured, in a sibling repo: a declared gate whose dependency was missing printed "SKIPPED" and
# exited 0 — "a declared gate that ALWAYS PASSED WITHOUT EXECUTING". A green row and a
# non-executing gate are indistinguishable to every reader downstream, so silence here would be
# the guard's-silence-reads-as-approval defect one layer up.
#
# ⚑⚑ IT GATES THE STAGED INDEX, NOT THE WORKING TREE. The two diverge exactly when `git add` is
# partial, which is the normal case — so a gate reading the working tree blesses bytes that are
# not the ones being committed.
#
# ⚑ NO FRAMEWORK. Plain executables under `core.hooksPath`, matching every peer repo in this
# ecosystem. Arm with:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks
set -euo pipefail

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE GATE RE-EXECS FROM AN IMMUTABLE SNAPSHOT OF ITSELF, BECAUSE `core.hooksPath` POINTS INTO
# THE WORKING TREE AND GIT RUNS THIS FILE LIVE. Measured: `git hash-object .githooks/pre-commit`
# equals its index entry, so the executed bytes ARE the tree's bytes — any edit to this file is
# visible to every party's in-flight commit, mid-write, with no atomicity.
#
# ⚑⚑ MEASURED ACROSS TWO TREES, 61 SECONDS APART (`substrate`'s gate-G86, corroborated here from
# the log). substrate's commit died at `.githooks/pre-commit:302`, *syntax error near `(`*, AFTER
# running 226+110+40 pytest and 35/35 bazel — and `bash -n` parsed the file clean a minute later:
#
#     e2883f9  16:29:40  substrate's leg landed
#     2a17b99  16:30:41  this file's residue-sweep repair committed
#
# **They read it mid-write.** ⚑ And it compounds: `2a17b99` IS the fix for a concurrency defect, so
# the repair for one produced a second one in the same file inside the same minute.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ NEITHER PARTY COULD SEE IT ALONE. substrate saw a parse error in a file it does not own; I
# saw a normal edit. **Only the two commit timestamps together say what happened**, and it does not
# reproduce afterward — so a later reader concludes the report was spurious. The general shape:
# *a gate whose own source lives in the tree it guards can be read mid-write, and the failure is
# attributed to whoever happened to be committing.*
#
# ⚑ ARMED BOTH WAYS: with the live file mutated to `this is a syntax error (`, the live file fails
# `bash -n` at that line and the snapshot parses clean.
if [ -z "${_GATE_SNAPSHOT:-}" ]; then
    _snap="${TMPDIR:-/home/mikemol/.cache}/mtools-gate-$$.sh"
    if cp "$0" "$_snap" 2>/dev/null; then
        export _GATE_SNAPSHOT="$_snap"
        # ⚑ NO `trap` HERE: it would fire when `exec` replaces this shell and delete the file
        # mid-read. The snapshot removes itself below, in the process that actually runs it.
        exec bash "$_snap" "$@"
    fi
    # ⚑ A FAILED COPY REPORTS AND CONTINUES. Refusing would turn a full tmpfs into a commit
    # refusal, and this is a rare-hazard protection rather than a correctness one — but a SILENT
    # fallback would hide that the protection is off, which is this repo's own defect class.
    printf 'pre-commit: ⚑ could not snapshot self to %s — running LIVE from the tree.\n' "$_snap" >&2
    printf '  an edit to this file mid-run may produce a syntax error blamed on your commit.\n' >&2
else
    # ⚑ SELF-DELETING, and deliberately not via a trap: this process IS the snapshot's image, so
    # unlinking now means no abandoned-run path can skip the cleanup — the exact defect the
    # residue sweep below exists to repair, one layer up.
    rm -f "$_GATE_SNAPSHOT"
fi

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

# ⚑⚑⚑ THIS GATE'S FAILURE SURFACE IS WIDER THAN YOUR COMMIT, AND IT SAYS SO BEFORE IT RUNS.
# Filed as BINDING by the constitution apex (`AX-03d`), against mtools' own filing of it as local.
# mtools recorded that the cost does not land only on mtools and kept the rule local anyway,
# reasoning that seven sessions sharing one working tree is a deployment fact rather than a
# property of the rule. That is sound for the RULE and left the binding half unfiled.
#
# The mechanism: the domain witnesses below mutate the REAL file, because a witness over a copy
# proves the copy's domain. So this gate reads the whole tree, and a peer's in-flight edit
# refuses YOUR scoped, correct commit. Measured: `paperkit` and `substrate` each had one refused
# by mtools' uncommitted mdstruct work, and both correctly declined `--no-verify`.
#
# ⚑⚑ SO BOTH PEERS PAID FOR A SCOPE THEY HAD NOT CONSENTED TO AND COULD NOT OBSERVE — cassian's
# `CO-L4` (*an actor that mutates state SHARED with parties who did not consent must not do so
# unilaterally*) arriving on a gate's refusal surface. The announcement below is the consent half:
# it cannot narrow the surface, and it makes it observable BEFORE THE SUITE RUNS rather than after,
# so a refusal naming someone else's file is legible instead of mystifying. ⚑ (This said `before
# the 130s is spent`; the announcement's value is that it precedes the work, not that the work
# takes any particular number of seconds.)
if [ -n "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^[MARCD] ' || true)" ]; then
    printf 'pre-commit: ⚑ THIS GATE READS THE WHOLE TREE, NOT YOUR PATHSPEC.\n'
    printf '  Unstaged changes are present. If this commit is refused for a file you did not\n'
    printf '  touch, that is this gate'"'"'s scope and not your defect — the domain witnesses\n'
    printf '  mutate real files, so the tree is the subject. Dirty, excluding your staged set:\n'
    git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^[MARCD] ' | sed 's/^/    /'
fi
cd "$root"

fail=0

# ⚑⚑⚑ WHAT FAILED IS RECORDED, NOT JUST THAT SOMETHING DID. `fail=1` collapses nineteen distinct
# checks to one bit, so the gate knew why it refused and could not say. Measured by a peer: a
# control failure sat TWENTY-TWO LINES above `REFUSED`, with every subsequent check printing green
# in between — baselines unchanged, roster 51/51, figure freshness clean, orphan check clean.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE INTERVENING GREENS ARE NOT NEUTRAL, THEY ARE PERSUASIVE. A reader looking at the tail
# sees a clean run that refuses for no stated reason, and the evidence for "everything is fine" is
# the LAST thing printed. That is this repository's own subject arriving in its output layer
# rather than its logic.
failed_checks=""
failed_detail=""
# ⚑⚑⚑ A REFUSAL THAT NAMES AN OUTCOME AND NOT A SUBJECT IS ONE NOBODY CAN CLEAR, AND THIS GATE
# SHIPPED ONE. `linux-sources` was refused by `shellcheck domain reaches every shell file` and
# reported that `grep shellcheck` over the ENTIRE run returned two lines: a passing bazel target
# and the refusal summary. No file, no rule, no count. ⚑ The witness DOES print three arms — but
# to stderr, interleaved into a 35-target bazel run hundreds of lines earlier, so the account
# exists and is unfindable. **Detail that exists somewhere and is not attached to the verdict is
# detail the reader does not have.**
#
# ⚑⚑ THE SAME SHAPE THIS ECOSYSTEM SPENT AN AFTERNOON ON, IN THE OTHER GATE. `linux-sources`'
# `//:gate` printed `gate verdict: fail` while fifteen per-claim records held `1 failing of 1` in
# one file and `0 of 1` in fourteen — *a two-second read against fifteen guesses*. Mine was worse:
# zero of unknown, because no per-check record exists to go read.
#
# ⚑ SO A FAILING CHECK CAPTURES ITS OWN OUTPUT AND REPLAYS IT UNDER THE VERDICT. The second
# argument is optional; where it is absent the label still lands, which keeps every existing call
# site correct rather than making 20 of them a prerequisite for the repair.
note_failure() {
    failed_checks="$failed_checks
  - $1"
    if [ -n "${2:-}" ] && [ -s "$2" ]; then
        failed_detail="$failed_detail
  --- $1
$(sed 's/^/      /' "$2")"
    fi
}
say() { printf 'pre-commit: %s\n' "$1" >&2; }

# ⚑⚑⚑ ONE HELPER, BECAUSE EIGHT CALL SITES WITH THE SAME REPAIR IS EIGHT PLACES TO FORGET IT.
# The shellcheck witness was wired to capture two commits ago and the other eight were not, so a
# refusal naming seven witnesses printed seven bare labels and then the gate's own honest line:
# *no check captured its own output.* ⚑ MEASURED FROM THE BLOCKED SEAT: `linux-sources`, refused
# five times, said that line is *why I looked at `state.py` instead of at my own diff* — the
# account being missing was itself actionable. But an actionable absence is still an absence.
#
# ⚑⚑ A REPAIR APPLIED TO ONE CALL SITE IS NOT A REPAIR TO THE CLASS. I wrote that sentence into
# the ratchet's wiring and then left eight witnesses unwired in the same file. This is the fix
# stated once so the next witness added inherits it.
# ⚑ THE LOG DIRECTORY IS CREATED ONCE, HERE, AND NOTHING ELSE OWNS IT. It is deliberately NOT
# `$staged`: see `witness()` below.
_witness_logs="${TMPDIR:-/home/mikemol/.cache}/mtools-gate-logs"
# ⚑⚑ THE RECORDER IS SHARED WITH `commit-msg`, which held four unrecorded refusal paths. Sourced
# rather than duplicated: this file's own `note_failure` comment says a repair applied to one call
# site is not a repair to the class, and two writers for one record is that shape.
if [ -r "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/refusal_record.sh" ]; then
    . "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/refusal_record.sh"
else
    record_refusal() { :; }
fi
mkdir -p "$_witness_logs"

witness() {  # label, then domain_witness.sh's own arguments
    _wlabel="$1"; shift
    # ⚑⚑⚑ THE LOGS LIVE OUTSIDE `$staged`, BECAUSE THAT DIRECTORY IS WIPED MID-RUN. `witness()` is
    # defined at ~142 and `rm -rf "$staged"; mkdir -p "$staged"` runs at ~283, so any witness log
    # written before the wipe is destroyed and any written after survives. ⚑ MEASURED by `gabion`
    # across six refusals: the `cat` failure named a DIFFERENT witness each run — whichever landed
    # on the wrong side — and `ls $staged/.witness-*` returned nothing at all, so it was never one
    # missing log. Not correlated with tree state, content, the lock, or residue, which is why
    # five separate mechanisms failed to predict it.
    #
    # ⚑⚑ AND IT IS THE TRAP-VERSUS-PRE-FLIGHT FINDING ONE FILE OVER, IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION.
    # There a cleanup ran too late; here an initialisation does. *A directory that is both a
    # scratch space and a log destination has two lifetimes and only one `rm -rf` line.*
    #
    # ⚑ SO THE LOGS GET THEIR OWN DIRECTORY RATHER THAN THE WIPE BEING MOVED — `gabion`'s
    # preference as a consumer, and the better one: **a log that explains a refusal should outlive
    # the scratch tree the refusal was about.** Moving the wipe would fix this ordering and leave
    # the next reordering free to break it again.
    _wlog="$_witness_logs/witness-$(printf '%s' "$_wlabel" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9' '_').log"
    if ./domain_witness.sh "$@" >"$_wlog" 2>&1; then
        cat "$_wlog" >&2
    else
        cat "$_wlog" >&2
        fail=1; note_failure "$_wlabel" "$_wlog"
    fi
}

# ⚑⚑⚑ A REFUSAL THAT NAMES ONLY ITS CLAIM MAKES THE BLOCKED PARTY DO THE DIAGNOSIS. Measured this
# afternoon: TWELVE peer refusals, six to `linux-sources` and six to `gabion`, and every single
# mechanism was found by the party the gate blocked rather than by the gate. The checks below
# stream to stderr, so a HUMAN at a terminal sees the detail — but `note_failure`'s summary is what
# a peer reads out of a captured run, and it carried the claim with none of the evidence.
#
# ⚑⚑ SO THE CAPTURE IS THE SAME SHAPE `witness()` ALREADY USES, HOISTED RATHER THAN RE-TYPED: run
# into a log, echo the log to stderr so the interactive path is unchanged, and hand the log to
# `note_failure` so the refusal REPLAYS the finding. Three sites repeating a pattern is how the
# ordering defect got in the first time; one helper is one place to get it right.
#
# ⚑ IT DOES NOT SUPPRESS OUTPUT AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A gate that swallowed stderr to tidy its
# summary would trade a legible refusal for a quiet one, which is the defect one layer over.
run_checked() {  # label, then the command
    _rlabel="$1"; shift
    _rclog="$_witness_logs/check-$(printf '%s' "$_rlabel" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9' '_').log"
    if "$@" >"$_rclog" 2>&1; then
        cat "$_rclog" >&2
    else
        cat "$_rclog" >&2
        fail=1; note_failure "$_rlabel" "$_rclog"
    fi
}

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE POPULATION IS DERIVED ONCE, AND IT WAS HAND-WRITTEN AT FOUR SITES IN THIS FILE.
# Each read `for dist in hooks mdstruct ratchet`. `fence` landed at cc3d301 and NONE of them saw
# it: its 27 test functions carried zero warrants because the 1:1 ledger below never looked, and
# its checkers never ran here at all. ⚑ THE GATE WAS CORRECT OVER THE WRONG SET — a correct
# result over a mis-named population passes every check that examines the result.
# ⚑⚑ MEASURED SCOPE, and the recorded one was wrong: NINE sites across this file, `preflight.sh`
# and `test_bar_fires.py`, not the six a note claimed. The count of the defect had the defect.
# ⚑ THE CRITERION IS NOT INVENTED HERE. `blockers.sh` already enumerates components as *a dir
# with a pyproject.toml*, arguing that a structural criterion cannot drift as the repo grows and
# that a landed component necessarily satisfies it. This is that, once, instead of four literals.
_dists=$(cd "$root" && printf '%s ' */pyproject.toml | sed 's#/pyproject.toml##g')
# ⚑ AND IT REFUSES AN EMPTY DERIVATION RATHER THAN LOOPING ZERO TIMES. A glob that matched
# nothing would make every check below a no-op and the gate would pass having examined nothing —
# green over an empty population, which is the shape this whole file exists to refuse.
case "$_dists" in
  ''|'*/pyproject.toml ')
    say "no distribution found by */pyproject.toml — cannot run the gate, commit refused"
    say "  an empty population makes every check below a no-op that reports green"
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

# ⚑ TOOL PRESENCE IS CHECKED BEFORE ANY CHECK RUNS, so a missing tool cannot masquerade as a pass.
for dist in $_dists; do
  for tool in ruff mypy python3; do
    if [ ! -x "$dist/.venv/bin/$tool" ]; then
      say "$dist/.venv/bin/$tool not found — cannot run the gate, commit refused"
      say "  a skip here would report green over a check that never executed"
      record_refusal pre-commit " - $dist/.venv/bin/$tool absent"
      exit 1
    fi
  done
done

# ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE SINGLETON TOOLS TOO, BECAUSE THE LOOP ABOVE IS PER-DISTRIBUTION AND THEY ARE NOT.
# `mikemol-ratchet` lives ONLY in `ratchet/.venv` — measured: it is absent from fence, hooks and
# mdstruct — so no iteration of the triple above ever covers it. It was guarded instead by
# `if [ -x ... ]`, which SKIPS SILENTLY: the preview-debt ratchet, the check that has refused most
# often here, would simply not run and the commit would report green.
# ⚑⚑ THAT IS THE RULE THIS FILE STATES SIXTY LINES ABOVE, BROKEN BY THIS FILE — *a skip here would
# report green over a check that never executed* — and it is substrate's `check_scratch_runtime.py`
# defect, which the harness union explicitly refuses as a SHAPE rather than as one gate.
# ⚑ THE ABSENCE PATH HAD NEVER BEEN EXERCISED: every guarded tool exists on this host, so the
# guards had only ever taken their true branch. `//hooks:test_bar_fires` now derives both
# populations from this file and refuses any `if [ -x ]` guard on a tool the loops do not cover.
# ⚑ WRITTEN AS A PLAIN CHECK, NOT A ONE-ELEMENT LOOP: shellcheck's SC2043 is right that a `for`
# over a single literal reads as a bad expansion. A second singleton tool gets its own check.
_singleton=ratchet/.venv/bin/mikemol-ratchet
if [ ! -x "$_singleton" ]; then
  say "$_singleton not found — cannot run the gate, commit refused"
  say "  a skip here would report green over a check that never executed"
  record_refusal pre-commit " - $_singleton absent"
  exit 1
fi

# ⚑ THE INDEX IS MATERIALISED SO THE CHECKS READ WHAT IS BEING COMMITTED, not what is on disk.
# ⚑⚑⚑ A STABLE PATH, NOT A TEMP DIR, BECAUSE BAZEL RUNS HERE TOO AND ITS OUTPUT BASE IS A HASH OF
# THE WORKSPACE PATH. Measured, full suite, three configurations — **stated as bazel's own counters
# rather than as elapsed time**:
#
#   working tree (warm)         152 action cache hits,   0 actions executed
#   fresh mktemp staging          0 action cache hits,  68 disk cache hits, 187 analysis actions
#   stable staging, 2nd run     action cache warm, analysis reused
#
# ⚑⚑ A NEW OUTPUT BASE IS NOT A COLD BUILD, AND THAT IS THE TRAP. The mktemp run reported
# `Executed 0 out of 34 tests` — no test reran, the disk cache carried every result — and still
# redid **187 processes of ANALYSIS**. The disk cache is content-addressed and crosses workspaces;
# the ACTION cache lives in the output base and does not.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE COUNTS ARE THE CLAIM AND THE SECONDS WERE DECORATION, WHICH IS THE CORRECTION.  This
# block previously read `14s / 99s / 104s / 5s`, and those figures are not properties of the gate —
# they are properties of one run's cache state, machine load, and whatever peers were committing
# concurrently. Operator: *wall time, even relative wall time, is not meaningful; using it in
# reasoning is demanding nondeterminism and hidden confounds.* ⚑ `187 analysis actions` and
# `0 action cache hits` are counters bazel emits, reproducible from the same cache state, and they
# carry the entire argument: a new output base discards the ACTION cache while keeping the DISK
# cache, so a run can execute nothing and still redo all analysis.
#
# ⚑ THE DIRECTORY IS NOT REMOVED ON EXIT, deliberately: removing it discards the action cache and
# forces analysis to be redone. It is refreshed from the index each run, so it never holds stale
# content.
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE GATE SWEEPS ITS OWN WITNESSES' RESIDUE, BECAUSE A REFUSAL WAS GENERATING THE NEXT ONE.
# Nine domain witnesses below mutate REAL tracked files — arm 2 plants a defect to prove the target
# refuses it, arm 3 restores. Each witness traps its own EXIT. **But the GATE has no trap**, so
# when an early check fails and the run is abandoned, witnesses die inside their mutation windows
# and their probes survive into the NEXT attempt, which refuses on residue rather than on content.
#
# ⚑⚑ MEASURED, TWICE IN ONE TICK. Attempt 1 left residue in four victims (`blockers.sh`,
# `cmdparse.py`, `frontmatter.py`, `state.py`) — one of them a TYPE defect, which produced six
# mypy/ruff refusals that read as six independent failures. Attempt 2, after clearing all four by
# hand, left residue in three more. **The refusal generates its own next refusal**, and each round
# strands sabotage for whoever commits next — which, with seven sessions writing this tree, is
# usually not the party that produced it.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND A PEER CORRECTLY WILL NOT CLEAR IT. `gabion` declined twice today to revert files it does
# not own, for the right reason: a peer cannot distinguish a witness's sabotage from the owner's
# in-flight work. So the only party who can clean it is the one whose gate made it, and that party
# has already exited. **The cleanup has to be the gate's, not a reader's.**
#
# ⚑ THIS SWEEPS ONLY WHAT CARRIES THE PROBE MARKER, never a file merely dirty. A blanket
# `git checkout` over the victims would discard a peer's real work in the window where this gate
# happens to run — the exact defect `domain_witness`'s own unstaged-victim guard exists to refuse.
_witness_victims="blockers.sh
hooks/src/mikemol/hooks/cmdparse.py
mdstruct/src/mikemol/mdstruct/frontmatter.py
ratchet/src/mikemol/ratchet/state.py
ratchet/ratchet-preview.txt"

# ⚑⚑ NO PIPELINE HERE, DELIBERATELY. A `printf | while read` puts the loop in a SUBSHELL, so a
# `git checkout` inside it cannot report failure to the caller and the sweep silently does nothing.
# MEASURED on the first cut of this function: residue planted, sweep run, residue still present,
# and the function printed no error. That is this repository's own pipeline-swallows-the-status
# defect — the one `hook_no_chaining` exists to refuse — committed inside the repair for a
# different silent failure.
sweep_witness_residue() {
    for v in $_witness_victims; do
        [ -n "$v" ] || continue
        # ⚑⚑⚑ BOTH SPELLINGS, BECAUSE ONE PAYLOAD CARRIED NEITHER MARKER AND WAS INVISIBLE HERE.
        # The mypy probe appended `def _transient_domain_probe()` with NO marker comment, so this
        # predicate skipped it while the other four victims swept cleanly. ⚑ MEASURED FROM TWO
        # SEATS: `linux-sources` reported four files swept with `state.py` absent from the list and
        # correctly declined to guess whether that was a missing path or a probe planted after the
        # sweep; `gabion` measured the same file by content hash and found a live probe. **It was
        # neither — the path was in the list and the predicate could not see the payload.**
        #
        # ⚑⚑ The payload now carries the marker too (`domain_witness.sh`), so this is belt AND
        # braces: a sweep keyed on one spelling of a thing with two spellings is the same
        # cheap-key defect this repository has now found in six instruments.
        git diff -- "$v" 2>/dev/null \
            | grep -qE 'transient domain probe|_transient_domain_probe' || continue
        # ⚑⚑⚑ THE THREE OUTCOMES ARE SEPARATED, AND CONFLATING THEM WAS THE MIRROR OF THE DEFECT
        # THIS SWEEP EXISTS TO REPAIR. The first cut ran `git checkout -- "$v" 2>/dev/null` and
        # branched on its status alone, so a failure to acquire `.git/index.lock` was reported as
        # `COULD NOT sweep probe residue` — indistinguishable from a real content failure.
        #
        # ⚑⚑ MEASURED BY A PEER, THEN REPRODUCED HERE. `gabion` was refused with three files named
        # unsweepable and found all three CLEAN seconds later; reproduced in a scratch repo with a
        # held lock: `git checkout` exits 128 and the file stays dirty, and nothing in the message
        # says which of the two happened. ⚑ Their statement of it is the one to keep: *arm 3 used
        # to say "the tree is dirty" when it meant "my residue survived"; this said "COULD NOT
        # sweep" when the residue was gone. The attribution was repaired and the polarity was then
        # wrong in the other direction.*
        #
        # ⚑ SO THE ERROR IS CAPTURED RATHER THAN DISCARDED, and the file is re-measured afterward.
        # A checkout that failed on the lock while another party's commit cleared the same residue
        # leaves the file CLEAN, which is a success this gate should not report as a failure.
        _sweep_err="$(git checkout -- "$v" 2>&1)"; _sweep_rc=$?
        if [ "$_sweep_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
            say "swept this gate's own probe residue from $v"
        elif ! git diff -- "$v" 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'transient domain probe'; then
            say "probe residue in $v is gone — the checkout failed but the file is clean"
            say "  (git said: ${_sweep_err%%$'\n'*})"
        else
            say "⚑ COULD NOT sweep probe residue from $v — run: git checkout -- $v"
            say "  git said: ${_sweep_err%%$'\n'*}"
        fi
    done
}
trap sweep_witness_residue EXIT

# ⚑⚑⚑ AND IT RUNS ONCE UP FRONT, BECAUSE AN EXIT TRAP CLEANS UP AFTER THE RUN IT COULD HAVE SAVED.
# MEASURED: a commit was refused by two witnesses, both reporting `already carries probe residue`
# on `cmdparse.py`, and the very next line of the same run read
# `swept this gate's own probe residue from hooks/src/mikemol/hooks/cmdparse.py`. ⚑ The sweep did
# its job — one run too late. The residue was a PREDECESSOR's, the trap fired at the end as
# designed, and the witnesses had already refused on a condition this gate was about to clear.
#
# ⚑⚑ SO THE TRAP AND THE PRE-FLIGHT ARE TWO DIFFERENT JOBS WEARING ONE NAME. The trap cleans up
# THIS run's own residue so the NEXT party is not blocked. This call clears a PREDECESSOR's so
# THIS run is not blocked by a corpse. Neither substitutes for the other, and having only the
# first meant every stranded probe cost exactly one extra full refusal before it was swept.
sweep_witness_residue

staged="${TMPDIR:-/home/mikemol/.cache}/mtools-staged"
rm -rf "$staged"; mkdir -p "$staged"
git checkout-index --all --prefix="$staged/"

for dist in $_dists; do
  [ -d "$staged/$dist" ] || continue
  say "checking $dist"
  # ⚑⚑⚑ `MYPYPATH` AND `PYTHONPATH` POINT AT THE STAGED TREE, AND WITHOUT THIS THE GATE REPORTED
  # ONE VERDICT OVER TWO POPULATIONS. The venv is an EDITABLE install — a `.pth` plus an
  # import-hook finder naming `$root/$dist/src` — so `cd $staged/$dist` changed the cwd and not
  # the import closure. MEASURED: a type defect planted in the WORKING tree, absent from the
  # staged copy, was still imported by a run inside the staged copy —
  #     staged run imports payload from /home/mikemol/github/mtools/hooks/src/... ; has leak? True
  # so mypy type-checked staged sources whose imports resolved to unstaged ones.
  #
  # ⚑⚑ THE HALF I FIXED WAS THE HALF I COULD SEE FROM THE SHELL. The tree-vs-index gap was closed
  # for the checkers three commits earlier, with a commit message about the gate's two halves
  # checking different code — and the import closure was left open, because `cd` looks like it
  # relocates a program and only relocates its cwd.
  #
  # ⚑ CREDIT: linux-sources, which hit the same class from the other direction — an UNDECLARED
  # INPUT present in an unsandboxed run and absent in a sandboxed one. Their statement is the
  # general form: *a green from the unsandboxed path is not evidence about the sandboxed one;
  # they are different populations.* An editable install naming a root and a missing declared
  # input are two mechanisms of one failure — the thing under test reaching outside its declared
  # closure, and the reader unable to tell.
  run_checked "$dist: ruff — lint clean under select=[ALL]" \
    env -C "$staged/$dist" \
        MYPYPATH="$staged/$dist/src:$staged/$dist/stubs" \
        PYTHONPATH="$staged/$dist/src" \
        "$root/$dist/.venv/bin/ruff" check --no-cache .
  run_checked "$dist: mypy — types clean, strict" \
    env -C "$staged/$dist" \
        MYPYPATH="$staged/$dist/src:$staged/$dist/stubs" \
        PYTHONPATH="$staged/$dist/src" \
        "$root/$dist/.venv/bin/mypy"
  # ⚑ TESTS RUN FROM THE REAL TREE: their venv holds absolute paths, and a witness that cannot
  # find its own interpreter is an infrastructure failure reported as a red gate.
  #
  # ⚑⚑ AND THIS IS THE THIRD POPULATION, LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY AND NAMED SO IT IS NOT MISTAKEN
  # FOR CLOSED. ruff and mypy now read the staged tree through `PYTHONPATH`; `bazel test //...`
  # below reads a sandbox built from declared `srcs`; this line reads the WORKING TREE. So a
  # pytest green covers what is on disk, not what is being committed, and the two diverge exactly
  # when `git add` is partial — which is the normal case.
  #
  # ⚑ IT IS NOT CLOSED BY POINTING THIS AT `$staged` BECAUSE THE BAZEL SUITE ALREADY COVERS THE
  # STAGED TREE HERMETICALLY, and a second staged-tree pytest would duplicate it while losing the
  # one thing this run has that bazel does not: the developer venv, where a missing dependency
  # surfaces as an import error rather than as a sandbox that never had it. The honest statement
  # is that this line is a DEVELOPER-TREE smoke check and the hermetic verdict is bazel's.
  run_checked "$dist: pytest — every case passes" \
    env -C "$root/$dist" .venv/bin/python3 -m pytest -q
done

# ⚑⚑ THE WARRANT LEDGER MUST STAY 1:1 AND CLOSED. A test arriving unwarranted is the membership
# failure this repo refuses: not a COUNT of unwarranted cases, but the SET — warranting one case
# while adding another leaves a count unchanged and the membership churned.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE POPULATION THIS 1:1 RANGES OVER IS TEST **FUNCTIONS**, NOT TEST **CASES**. Measured:
# `hooks` holds 176 `def test_` lines and `pytest --collect-only` collects **248** — 15
# `parametrize` decorators across four modules expand the difference. ⚑ The invariant is correct as
# designed (warrants are per-function; the plan settled that), and the COUNT is right. What is
# wrong is the sentence a reader takes from it: this ledger does NOT say *every test case is
# warranted*, it says *every test function is*. **248 cases run; 176 carry a warrant.**
# ⚑ A correct count over a MIS-NAMED population is harder to catch than a wrong count, because
# every arithmetic check passes. Naming it here rather than changing the invariant, which is the
# operator's call and not a defect.
for dist in $_dists; do
  [ -d "$staged/$dist" ] || continue
  # ⚑⚑⚑ ANCHORED, BECAUSE AN UNANCHORED `@misc{` COUNTS A QUOTATION AS A WARRANT. This is a corpus
  # whose `claim` fields QUOTE CODE, so a warrant claiming something about bibtex syntax puts the
  # entry delimiter inside a field and the ledger reads 177 against 176. ⚑ MEASURED on a fixture:
  # one entry whose claim contains the string `@misc{` counts as **2** unanchored and **1**
  # anchored. The gate would then refuse every commit in the distribution with a bare arithmetic
  # disagreement and NO POINTER to the quotation that caused it.
  # ⚑⚑ IT AGREED WITH THE ANCHORED FORM AT 176 ON THE DAY IT WAS WRITTEN, which is exactly the
  # property that made it invisible — `rosettapkg` named the mechanism (*a substring frequency
  # offered as a count of kinds*) and it found this within minutes of being pointed at my gate.
  w=$(grep -c '^@misc{' "$staged/$dist/warrants.bib")
  # ⚑ `cat` then a single `grep -c`, NOT `grep -c` over the glob: -c counts PER FILE and would emit
  # one number per test module rather than the suite's total. shellcheck's SC2126 suggests a swap
  # that is wrong here — the aggregate is the point.
  # ⚑⚑⚑ COUNTED PER FILE, NOT OVER A CONCATENATION. `cat a b | grep -c '^def test_'` welds `a`'s
  # last line to `b`'s first, so a definition at the start of the next file stops matching the
  # anchor. MEASURED on a two-file fixture where the first lacks its trailing newline: the
  # per-file sum reads 3 and the concatenation reads 2.
  # ⚑⚑ AND THE ERROR IS THE REFUSING DIRECTION — fewer test functions than exist, so a ledger that
  # is genuinely 1:1 looks like it carries surplus warrants and the commit is refused, while
  # `preflight.sh` (which already counts per file) predicts a clean tree.
  # ⚑ ALL TEST MODULES END IN A NEWLINE TODAY, so the two agreed. That is the accident holding
  # rather than the property being enforced. Per-file counting is correct independent of file
  # endings; a lint rule forbidding the missing newline would fix the ACCIDENT and leave the
  # reader wrong — a second thing to keep true instead of one thing that cannot go wrong.
  # ⚑⚑⚑ PARSED, NOT MATCHED, AND A GREP COUNTED TEST SOURCE QUOTED AS DATA. This read
  # `grep -h -cE '^(    )?def test_'`. `hooks/tests/test_grade.py` builds its fixture arms as an
  # f-string containing `def test_behavioural()` — test source quoted so the grader under test
  # has something to grade. MEASURED over that file: `ast.walk` 17, `pytest --collect-only` 17,
  # the grep 20 — three lines inside a string literal. The ledger is 1:1, so those three could
  # only ever be satisfied by warrants for things that do not exist.
  # ⚑⚑ AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS CONFINED, which is what makes the diagnosis a diagnosis: parse
  # and grep agree exactly on `fence` (33), `mdstruct` (100) and `ratchet` (39), and differ on
  # `hooks` by precisely 3. A parse that disagreed everywhere would indicate a bug in the parse.
  # ⚑ THIS IS THE `@misc{` DEFECT ONE FIELD OVER, already recorded above: an artifact that quotes
  # its own subject cannot be measured by matching that subject's syntax.
  t=$("$root/count_test_functions.py" "$staged/$dist")
  if [ "$w" -ne "$t" ]; then
    say "$dist: $w warrants against $t test functions — every test must arrive warranted"
    # ⚑⚑ THE NUMBERS GO INTO THE RECORD, NOT ONLY ONTO stderr. `say` reaches a human at a
    # terminal; `note_failure`'s LABEL is what the refusal summary and `REFUSALS.tsv` carry, and a
    # bare label writes a row saying *the ledger disagreed* without saying by how much or which
    # way. ⚑ The two counts already exist here — withholding them from the record was a choice
    # nobody made deliberately.
    _wlog="$_witness_logs/warrant-ledger-$dist.log"
    printf '%s warrants against %s test functions (parametrize expands cases beyond functions)\n' \
        "$w" "$t" > "$_wlog"
    fail=1; note_failure "$dist: warrant ledger 1:1" "$_wlog"
  fi
  if ! diff -q \
      <(grep -o 'section = {[^}]*}' "$staged/$dist/warrants.bib" | sed 's/.*{\(.*\)}/\1/' | sort -u) \
      <(grep -v '^#' "$staged/$dist/rubric.tsv" | cut -f1 | sort -u) >/dev/null; then
    say "$dist: warrant sections and rubric sections disagree"
    # ⚑ THE DIFF IS THE FINDING AND IT WAS DISCARDED. `diff -q` was run for its status alone, so
    # the refusal named a disagreement and never said WHICH sections. Re-run without `-q` into the
    # record: a reader gets the symmetric difference rather than the fact that one exists.
    _slog="$_witness_logs/warrant-sections-$dist.log"
    diff <(grep -o 'section = {[^}]*}' "$staged/$dist/warrants.bib" | sed 's/.*{\(.*\)}/\1/' | sort -u) \
         <(grep -v '^#' "$staged/$dist/rubric.tsv" | cut -f1 | sort -u) > "$_slog" 2>&1 || true
    fail=1; note_failure "$dist: warrant sections vs rubric sections" "$_slog"
  fi
done

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE HERMETIC SUITE IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE DEPENDENCIES ARE DECLARED. The host runs
# above use each distribution's own `.venv`, which can satisfy an import nobody declared — a
# green suite there says nothing about whether a consumer installing the wheel gets a working
# package. Under bazel the sandbox stages ONLY what the BUILD file names, and this caught exactly
# that on its first run: five mdstruct witnesses failed on `No module named panflute` because the
# py_library declared no deps. The host suite had been green throughout.
#
# ⚑ IT REFUSES WHEN BAZEL IS ABSENT, like every other tool here. A skip would report green over a
# suite that never ran, which is the defect this file exists to refuse.
if ! command -v bazel >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  say "bazel not found — cannot run the hermetic suite, commit refused"
  record_refusal pre-commit " - bazel absent"
  exit 1
fi
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE SUITE NAMES ITS FAILING TARGETS, because a label is only as specific as the check under
# it. A peer read a real refusal and found every label actionable except this one: "the hermetic
# suite" is a claim, but a suite is MANY claims, so on failure it collapses them exactly as
# `fail=1` collapsed nineteen checks. ⚑ That was the granularity bound, not a defect in the
# labels — and bazel already prints `//pkg:target   FAILED`, so the information existed and was
# being discarded one line from where it was needed.
# ⚑⚑ THE STATUS IS CAPTURED, NOT PIPED. `if ! bazel ... | tee` reads TEE's status, and measured
# here a failing left side is LOST without `pipefail` — a green over a failed suite, inside the
# fix for a labelling bound. This file does set `pipefail` at the top, so the piped form would
# have worked; it would have worked BY A SETTING THREE SECTIONS AWAY rather than by construction,
# and a future edit that scoped that setting would break this silently.
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND `set -e` KILLS A BARE FAILING PIPELINE BEFORE THE CHECK BELOW RUNS. Measured: with a
# planted defect the gate exited 3 — bazel's own code — and printed no attributed refusal at all,
# because the script was already dead. ⚑ The repair for one pipeline hazard introduced another:
# `PIPESTATUS` is only readable if control reaches the line that reads it. `|| true` exempts the
# pipeline from `-e` while `PIPESTATUS[0]` still carries bazel's verdict.
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE SUITE RUNS IN THE STAGED TREE, so both halves of this gate check the same code. Host
# ruff and mypy already ran in `$staged`; `bazel test` did not, and measured on one planted defect
# the two halves returned OPPOSITE verdicts in a single run — host mypy said "Success: no issues
# found in 8 source files" while `//ratchet:mypy` said FAILED. ⚑ A gate that disagrees with itself
# about what its subject is has no verdict to give.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE WITNESSES STILL READ THE WORKING TREE, AND THAT IS NOT A GAP IN THIS FIX. Armed with a
# working-tree-only defect: the suite did NOT refuse (this change working), and a domain witness
# DID — its control found the target red before the probe and declined to interpret its arms.
# ⚑ A witness proves a domain by mutating the REAL file and restoring it; a witness over a copy
# would prove the copy's domain. So the witnesses are irreducibly working-tree-scoped, and a
# peer's dirty file can still refuse a commit through them. That is one row of a three-row class:
# the sweep (held by `git commit -- <path>`), a gate reading the tree (closed by staging), and a
# witness mutating real files (open, and closable only by a lock).
( cd "$staged" && bazel test //... --test_output=errors ) 2>&1 | tee "$staged/.suite.log" || true
if [ "${PIPESTATUS[0]}" -ne 0 ]; then
    failed_targets=$(grep -oE '^//[^ ]+' "$staged/.suite.log" 2>/dev/null | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')
    fail=1
    note_failure "the hermetic suite: ${failed_targets:-no target named — see the log above}"
fi

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE DOMAIN WITNESS RUNS HERE AND NOT AS A BAZEL ACTION, DELIBERATELY. It probes whether a
# checker's DECLARED domain equals its REAL one, which it can only do by mutating a source file and
# re-invoking bazel — so it is bazel-in-bazel, non-hermetic, and the sandbox has no bazel in it.
# Host tier is the honest placement; pretending otherwise would put an unrunnable target in the
# graph and call it covered.
#
# ⚑⚑ IT IS THE ONLY CHECK HERE THAT CAN CATCH A GREEN OVER A SHORT DOMAIN. Every other gate above
# asks "does the declared input set pass?" — this one asks whether that set is the right set. A
# `srcs` narrowed in a refactor, a glob that stopped matching, or a distribution added without the
# pattern all leave every other check green.
# ⚑⚑ ALL THREE DISTRIBUTIONS, NOT ONE. Arming a single distribution and leaving two carrying the
# identical unarmed claim is this repository's own "a control proves the QUERY works, never that the
# SEARCH SPACE was right" — the witness would report green over 1/3 of the corpus while 2/3 asserted
# an untested property. The population here is enumerated from the distributions that exist, and
# each victim is a genuinely TRANSITIVE module rather than a leaf:
#
#   ratchet   state.py       <- core.py <- cli.py
#   mdstruct  frontmatter.py <- pandoc.py <- ast.py <- tables.py <- cli.py   (four hops)
#   hooks     cmdparse.py    <- structural_query.py
#
# ⚑ A LEAF WOULD PASS ARM 1 FOR THE WRONG REASON: a directly-named file is trivially an input, so
# the arm would confirm the declaration rather than the CLOSURE. The victim must be a file the
# checker reaches only by following imports.
say "domain witness: each mypy target's declared domain vs its real one"
witness "ratchet: mypy domain reaches a transitive import" ratchet  //ratchet:mypy  ratchet/src/mikemol/ratchet/state.py
witness "mdstruct: mypy domain reaches a transitive import" mdstruct //mdstruct:mypy mdstruct/src/mikemol/mdstruct/frontmatter.py
witness "hooks: mypy domain reaches a transitive import" hooks    //hooks:mypy    hooks/src/mikemol/hooks/cmdparse.py

# ⚑⚑ RUFF TOO, AND THE PROBE KIND IS EXPLICIT. Three ticks armed mypy's domain across three
# distributions and left ruff's untested — "weaker claim" is not "tested claim". The 4th argument
# names the defect class to plant, because arm 2 cannot distinguish A DOMAIN THAT EXCLUDES THE FILE
# from A PROBE THIS CHECKER DOES NOT SEEK. Measured: the mypy probe against `//ratchet:ruff` fails
# arm 2 and reads exactly like a short domain.
#
# ⚑⚑ COST IS ASYMMETRIC AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A STOPWATCH READING. A mypy witness
# re-analyses its whole import closure per action; a ruff witness is per-file. So adding ruff
# witnesses scales with FILES TOUCHED while adding mypy witnesses scales with CLOSURE SIZE, and a
# linear projection from one to the other is wrong by that ratio.
#
# ⚑ THIS COMMENT PREVIOUSLY SAID `~13s`, `~1s`, `~3s` and `~26s`. Those are not properties of the
# witnesses — they are properties of one machine at one moment, and quoting them invites exactly
# the projection the sentence exists to refuse. The structural claim survives any machine; the
# seconds do not survive the next commit by a peer.
witness "ratchet: ruff domain reaches a transitive import" ratchet  //ratchet:ruff  ratchet/src/mikemol/ratchet/state.py  ruff
witness "mdstruct: ruff domain reaches a transitive import" mdstruct //mdstruct:ruff mdstruct/src/mikemol/mdstruct/frontmatter.py ruff
witness "hooks: ruff domain reaches a transitive import" hooks    //hooks:ruff    hooks/src/mikemol/hooks/cmdparse.py   ruff

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE LAST UNARMED CHECKER, AND IT GUARDS THIS FILE. mypy, ruff and the ratchet each have a
# domain witness across three distributions; `shellcheck_githooks` had none — so the one target
# that checks EVERY shell file, including the gate you are reading, was asserting a domain nobody
# had tested. Its population became a `glob()` two ticks ago precisely because a hand list had
# drifted to 7-of-11, which makes the untested domain the more consequential half.
# ⚑ THE WITNESS THAT REFUSED A PEER WITH NO ACCOUNT. Its arms go to stderr and vanish into a
# 35-target bazel run; captured here so the refusal carries them.
wlog="$staged/.witness-shellcheck.log"
if ! ./domain_witness.sh . //:shellcheck_githooks blockers.sh shellcheck >"$wlog" 2>&1; then
    cat "$wlog" >&2
    fail=1; note_failure "shellcheck domain reaches every shell file" "$wlog"
else
    cat "$wlog" >&2
fi

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE RATCHET GATE, AND ITS BASELINE AS A SEPARATE VICTIM. `ratchet_check.sh`'s own header
# warns that "a ratchet whose baseline sits outside its own key can be lowered with no gate
# noticing" — the baseline IS correctly declared in that target's `data`, and until now nothing
# proved it. These are two different domain claims about one target: the SOURCES it censuses, and
# the BASELINE it compares against.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE BASELINE ARM REMOVES A KEY RATHER THAN ADDING ONE, AND THE DIRECTION IS THE FINDING.
# Measured: appending a fabricated key re-executes and PASSES, correctly — a baseline holding a key
# the census does not produce is paydown. Only a REMOVED key leaves a real finding unaccounted. An
# arm built on the intuitive direction would have reported FAILED against a gate working exactly as
# designed.
witness "ratchet gate domain reaches its census sources" ratchet //ratchet:ratchet_gate ratchet/src/mikemol/ratchet/state.py ratchet
witness "ratchet gate domain reaches its BASELINE" ratchet //ratchet:ratchet_gate ratchet/ratchet-preview.txt          baseline

# ⚑⚑ FRESHNESS REPORTS, IT DOES NOT REFUSE, AND THE `|| true` IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN LAZY. Its
# subject is a shared cluster this repository does not own: a red gate on someone else's outage
# blocks commits unrelated to it, and a gate that blocks for reasons its author cannot fix is a
# gate that gets bypassed — which trades one stale rule for a disarmed harness. The output is the
# product; the exit status is not.
say "rule freshness: are the environment claims this repo cites still true?"
./rule_freshness.sh || true

# ⚑⚑ FIGURES DECAY DIFFERENTLY FROM ENDPOINTS, so this is a second checker rather than a mode of
# the first. `rule_freshness.sh` re-measures the WORLD — a port answers or it does not. This
# re-reads THIS FILE for an invariant between two of its own statements: when a later rule writes
# "Rule N recorded <figure>", it has declared N's number historical, and N is then obliged to say
# so. ⚑ No clock, no cluster, no re-measurement — which is why it can run every commit while the
# thing it guards (a 132-second gate) cannot be re-timed inside itself.
say "figure freshness: is any superseded number still stated in the present tense?"
# ⚑⚑ ONE INVOCATION, AND IT ENUMERATES ITS OWN POPULATION. The gate named three documents by hand
# against seven this repository owns — the denylist defect fixed twice already here (a shellcheck
# target at 7 of 11, an `exports_files` at 8 of 13) reappearing in the checker built to catch stale
# claims. ⚑ The four unlisted files happened to be clean; the list would have missed them either
# way, which is luck rather than coverage.
./figure_freshness.sh || true
# ⚑⚑ THE DERIVATION FILE IS CHECKED TOO, and it is the one that needed it. The findings document
# recorded a retraction while `PATHS-FORWARD.md` — the file a session reads FIRST, to decide what
# to work on — kept asserting the falsified claim for nine ticks. ⚑ A correction that reaches the
# record without reaching the plan changes what is TRUE without changing what gets DONE.

# ⚑⚑ AN UNCLAIMED CHECKER REFUSES, WHERE A STALE ENVIRONMENT CLAIM ONLY REPORTS. The difference is
# ownership: freshness measures a shared cluster this repo does not control, so blocking on it
# would block commits their author cannot fix. An orphaned script is entirely inside this tree —
# it is a file somebody added and nothing runs, which is the green-over-nothing this whole harness
# exists to refuse, and the fix is always available to whoever tripped it.
say "orphan check: is every shell checker invoked, or declared as parked?"
# ⚑ CAPTURED, because the orphan it names is the whole content of the refusal. A bare label says
# *some checker is unaccounted for* and the reader must re-run the script to learn which.
run_checked "every shell checker is invoked or declared parked" ./orphan_check.sh

# ⚑⚑ THE ROSTER IS THE ONE POPULATION IN THIS REPOSITORY THAT CANNOT BE ENUMERATED, measured
# rather than assumed: PATH yields 2,843 executables and flagging all of it is useless; an
# `apropos` search for "text" returns 86 hits and MISSES all six readers added by hand, because
# `hexdump`'s own man page says "display file contents" and never "text". So the roster stays
# hand-written and what gets checked is DRIFT — an entry naming a tool this host does not have is
# a guess nobody can verify.
say "roster drift: does the textual roster still match the tools present?"
./roster_drift.sh || true

# ⚑⚑⚑ THIS FILE IS CHECKED BY `bazel test //:shellcheck_githooks`, WHICH IS RUN ABOVE. Until that
# target existed, the shell that runs every other check in this repository was verified by nothing
# — clean only because its author ran shellcheck by hand after each edit. A convention held in one
# person's memory, guarding the file that guards everything else. The checker's own script is in
# the same target's file list, because a gate that checks every shell file except itself leaves
# exactly the one nobody would think to look at.

# ⚑⚑ A STUB IS AN UNCHECKED CLAIM UNTIL SOMETHING CHECKS IT. mdstruct hand-writes a partial stub
# for panflute, and the stub's own prose cited a `scripts/check_stub_authority.py` that DID NOT
# EXIST — so the file claimed a verification nobody had built. stubtest is that verification, and
# on its first run it found two real divergences in the two functions this package calls most:
# `load`'s parameter was named `source` where the runtime calls it `input_stream`, and
# `stringify` was missing `newlines` entirely.
# ⚑ NO `if [ -x mdstruct/.venv/bin/python3 ]` GUARD: the per-distribution loop above already
# REFUSES the commit when any distribution's `python3` is missing, and mdstruct is in `$_dists`
# (derived from `*/pyproject.toml`). The guard was a redundant SECOND answer to a question already
# answered loudly — and a silent one, so where the two disagreed the silence would win.
# ⚑ `--concise` EXISTS TO MAKE THIS READABLE AND THE READER NEVER SAW IT. stubtest names the
# exact symbol whose signature drifted; the bare label said only that the stub disagreed.
run_checked "stubtest — the panflute stub still matches its module" \
  env -C mdstruct MYPYPATH=stubs .venv/bin/python3 -m mypy.stubtest panflute \
    --ignore-missing-stub --allowlist stubs/panflute-allowlist.txt --concise

# ⚑⚑ THE PREVIEW-RULE DEBT IS RATCHETED, PAYDOWN-ONLY, OVER A SET. Blanket `--preview` finds
# 192 findings this bar does not yet enforce; collapsing them to `file:rule` keys gives 62. The
# baseline is a SET, so paying one key down while another regresses is REFUSED even though the
# count is unchanged — the substitution a count-based gate cannot see. It runs against the REAL
# tree rather than the staged copy, because the census keys are paths and a staged copy's paths
# are not the ones a reader would go fix.
# ⚑ NO `if [ -x ]` GUARD HERE ANY MORE: the tool's presence is REFUSED ON above, so reaching this
# line means it exists. A guard would be a second, silent answer to a question already answered
# loudly — and it was the one that let an absent ratchet report green.
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE RATCHET CAPTURES ITS OWN OUTPUT, AND THE GATE NAMED THIS GAP ABOUT ITSELF FIRST.
# Refusing a commit last tick, it printed the two offending keys and then:
#     ⚑ no check captured its own output — the label above is all the account there is.
#       That is a defect in the CHECK, not in your commit: scroll for its name.
# ⚑ That is the gap-naming repair working as designed — converting a SILENCE into a NAMED gap —
# and the check it named is the one that has refused most often today: two paydowns of mine, a
# peer's fourth commit, and three earlier rounds. Six refusals, four parties.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE DETAIL ALWAYS EXISTED. `mikemol-ratchet` prints `N new key(s) REFUSED` and lists them
# by `path:rule`. It went to the terminal, hundreds of lines above the verdict and interleaved
# with a 35-target bazel run — so a refused party had the keys and could not find them, which is
# the same *detail that exists somewhere is detail the reader does not have* that the shellcheck
# witness was wired for two commits earlier. **A repair applied to one call site is not a repair
# to the class**, and this is the second of the twenty sites to be wired.
for dist in $_dists; do
  rlog="$staged/.ratchet-$dist.log"
  if ! ratchet/.venv/bin/mikemol-ratchet "$root/$dist" >"$rlog" 2>&1; then
      cat "$rlog" >&2
      fail=1; note_failure "$dist: ratchet — paydown only, no new keys" "$rlog"
  else
      cat "$rlog" >&2
  fi
done

# ⚑⚑⚑ THE INSTRUMENT ASSERTS ITS OWN CONTRACT BEFORE ITS OUTPUT IS TRUSTED. mdstruct silently
# dropped headings while `lint` and `roundtrip` both reported green on the corrupted file, so a
# bounded write against the parent landed inside the missing child. Every committed markdown file
# is checked with the tool's own verify mode — including this repo's own documents, which is where
# the defect was found.
# ⚑ NO `if [ -x mdstruct/.venv/bin/python3 ]` GUARD: the per-distribution loop above already
# REFUSES the commit when any distribution's `python3` is missing, and mdstruct is in `$_dists`.
# The guard was a redundant SECOND answer to a question already answered loudly — and a silent
# one, so where the two disagreed the silence would win.
while IFS= read -r md; do
  [ -f "$staged/$md" ] || continue
  # ⚑⚑ `verify` NAMES THE SWALLOWED HEADING AND ITS LINE, AND THIS DISCARDED IT TO /dev/null.
  # The refusal said a document had unreachable headings and made the author re-run the tool to
  # learn WHICH — for the one check whose whole output is a list of specific lines.
  _mdlog="$_witness_logs/mdverify-$(printf '%s' "$md" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9' '_').log"
  ( cd mdstruct && .venv/bin/python3 -m mikemol.mdstruct.cli verify "$staged/$md" >"$_mdlog" 2>&1 ) \
    || { say "$md: headings unreachable to mdstruct — a bounded write would land in the wrong section"
         cat "$_mdlog" >&2
         fail=1; note_failure "$md: headings unreachable to mdstruct" "$_mdlog"; }
  # ⚑⚑⚑ A RAGGED ROW IS THE ONE STRUCTURAL DEFECT NO AST READER HERE CAN SEE, AND THIS IS THE
  # ONLY MOMENT IT IS CATCHABLE. Pandoc pads a short row to its header's width before the AST
  # exists, so `mdstruct tables` reports a broken table as clean — measured, and asserted there
  # as a limit rather than repaired. Only the line-based shape linter can read it.
  # ⚑⚑ AND ONLY THE RECEIVING REPOSITORY'S GATE RUNS WHEN THE BYTES LAND. A peer's arm has been
  # finding these in this tree from outside, and they measured why that is not enough: a peer
  # file is swept at THEIR commit, strictly later than the write and often later than someone
  # else's repair. Every party here writes rows into every other party's censuses.
  # ⚑ AGAINST HEAD, NOT ABSOLUTE. Fifteen ragged rows exist across thirteen files, several in
  # FROZEN censuses and two in this session's own filed legs — none of which may be amended.
  # An absolute refusal would block every commit touching them, which is the arm-over-a-red-tree
  # hazard this repo records. The refusal fires on an INCREASE.
  # ⚑ A COUNT, AND ITS WEAKNESS IS STATED: moving a ragged row while adding another elsewhere
  # holds the count and passes. Set membership is stronger and belongs with the ratchet; this
  # catches the case the gate exists for — a row that was not ragged becoming ragged.
  # ⚑⚑ THE LINTER'S OUTPUT IS CAPTURED TO A FILE, NOT PIPED. `lint` exits 1 when it has
  # findings, so `| grep -c` would report grep's status and mask a CRASHED linter as a clean
  # document — a false zero, from the instrument, in the check built to catch false reads.
  # ⚑⚑⚑ `|| true`, AND WITHOUT IT THIS CHECK NEVER RAN. `lint` exits 1 whenever it has any
  # finding, and under `set -e` a bare failing command kills the script before the next line
  # reads `$?` — so the rc capture built to handle a nonzero status was unreachable for exactly
  # the case it exists for. MEASURED: a planted ragged row made the gate exit 1 with NO
  # attributed refusal, because the script was already dead. ⚑⚑ This file records that same
  # hazard at line 481, with this same remedy, about a different pipeline — a recorded lesson
  # is not an applied one, and here the record and the defect are in one file.
  _rag_rc=0
  ( cd mdstruct && .venv/bin/python3 -m mikemol.mdstruct.cli lint "$staged/$md" ) \
    >"$_mdlog.now" 2>&1 || _rag_rc=$?
  if [ "$_rag_rc" -gt 1 ]; then
    say "$md: the shape linter FAILED (rc=$_rag_rc) — a count from a crashed reader is not zero"
    cat "$_mdlog.now" >&2
    fail=1; note_failure "$md: shape linter rc=$_rag_rc" "$_mdlog.now"
  else
    _rag_now=$(grep -c MD056 "$_mdlog.now" || true)
    _rag_head=0
    if git show "HEAD:$md" >"$_mdlog.head" 2>/dev/null; then
      ( cd mdstruct && .venv/bin/python3 -m mikemol.mdstruct.cli lint "$_mdlog.head" ) \
        >"$_mdlog.was" 2>&1 || true
      _rag_head=$(grep -c MD056 "$_mdlog.was" || true)
    fi
    if [ "${_rag_now:-0}" -gt "${_rag_head:-0}" ]; then
      say "$md: ragged table row(s) — $_rag_now staged against $_rag_head in HEAD"
      say "  a raw or escaped pipe inside a code span splits the cell; every structural reader"
      say "  STOPS at that row and reports what it saw as complete. Escaping does NOT help —"
      say "  a field-splitting reader splits on the raw byte and an escaped pipe still ends it."
      grep MD056 "$_mdlog.now" >&2 || true
      fail=1; note_failure "$md: $_rag_now ragged row(s), was $_rag_head" "$_mdlog.now"
    fi
  fi
done < <(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM -- '*.md')

# ⚑ THE CAUSE IS RESTATED AT THE POINT OF REFUSAL, because that is where a reader is looking.
# A refusal that does not name its cause is read as a glitch, and a glitch is what gets bypassed.
if [ "$fail" -ne 0 ]; then
    # ⚑⚑⚑ THE REFUSAL IS RECORDED, BECAUSE THE DATA I CLAIMED EXISTED DID NOT. Two of this
    # repository's own census legs (`MT-06` row 1, `MT-K4`) state that the gate emits a refusal
    # account on every failure and nothing aggregates them — filed as *the population was never
    # named*, with the data said to be present. ⚑ MEASURED: the log directory holds **17 files, 0
    # containing a refusal**, because every run OVERWRITES them. There was no corpus to aggregate.
    #
    # ⚑⚑ SO THE MISSING PIECE WAS RETENTION, NOT A READER, and I had named the wrong one twice.
    # A reader built against that claim would have found an empty population and reported zero
    # peer refusals — a confident answer over a corpus that does not exist, which is this session's
    # own recurring defect arriving in the repair for it.
    #
    # ⚑ APPEND-ONLY, ONE LINE PER REFUSAL, NO PRUNING. A rotation policy is a decision about what
    # may be forgotten and this gate has no standing to make it; growth is bounded by how often
    # the gate refuses, which is the quantity being measured.
    # ⚑⚑⚑ THE PARTY IS THE SESSION, NOT THE COMMITTER. Every party in this shared tree commits as
    # the SAME `user.email` — MEASURED: `mikemol@gmail.com` for all of them, so the field this
    # record originally carried was **structurally incapable of naming who was refused.** The
    # record was built to answer *how many peer refusals has this gate caused*, and it recorded a
    # constant. ⚑ *A column that is the same for every row is a column that was never asked.*
    # ⚑⚑ `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` IS IN THE HOOK'S ENVIRONMENT AND DISTINGUISHES. Measured: this
    # session reads `0709aae6-…` and is stable across reads; a different session reads its own.
    # Both arms run before this was written — the email identical across two simulated parties,
    # the session id different.
    # ⚑ THE COMMITTER IS KEPT ANYWAY, and not because it discriminates. It does not. It is the
    # identity the commit will actually carry, so a reader comparing this record against `git log`
    # needs it to join on; dropping it would make the row true and unjoinable.
    # ⚑ ONE WRITER. This was an inline block; `commit-msg`'s four refusal paths could not reach
    # it, so six of seven paths recorded nothing. The shared recorder carries the header, the
    # session column, the hook column and the detail file — all measured here first.
    record_refusal pre-commit "$failed_checks" "${failed_detail:-}"
    say "REFUSED — the following check(s) failed:${failed_checks:-
  (unattributed: a check set the failure flag without naming itself)}"
    # ⚑ THE ACCOUNT IS REPLAYED UNDER THE VERDICT, not left where it was printed. A reader who
    # greps for the failing check's name must find its arms next to it rather than several
    # hundred interleaved bazel lines earlier.
    if [ -n "$failed_detail" ]; then
        say "the failing check(s) said:$failed_detail"
    else
        say "  ⚑ no check captured its own output — the label above is all the account there is."
        say "    That is a defect in the CHECK, not in your commit: scroll for its name."
    fi
    exit 1
fi
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE VERDICT IS AN EXPLICIT `exit 0`, NOT AN `echo`'s STATUS. The last line of a script is
# its exit code, and this line was `say "ok"` — a `printf` that succeeds unconditionally. The gate
# was CORRECT, because the refusal path `exit 1`s above; but the success path was reporting
# "printf worked", and those two agree only by luck of the control flow.
#
# ⚑⚑ MEASURED, because a caller two seats away paid for this shape. `rosettapkg` reported a
# backgrounded run that printed `All checks passed!`, exited 0, and left its file STAGED AND
# UNCOMMITTED — two runs, same exit status, opposite outcomes. Reproduced here:
#
#     for i in 1 2 3; do ./gate.sh; done            -> rc=1   the work's status survives
#     for i in 1 2 3; do ./gate.sh || true; done
#     echo "done"                                    -> rc=0   the TRAILING COMMAND won
#
# ⚑ THE GENERAL FORM IS NOT ABOUT PIPES OR LOOPS: **the last command wins, and a wrapper's last
# command is almost never the work.** A pipe reports its last stage, a loop reports its last
# iteration, a script reports its last line. Same rule, three surfaces — `gabion`'s formulation
# after hitting the third instance in one day.
#
# ⚑ SO THIS FILE ENDS ON A DECISION. `exit 0` cannot be reached by a stray `echo` appended later,
# which is the reversion this guards against: the defect was not that the code was wrong, it was
# that the code was RIGHT BY ACCIDENT and nothing said so.
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE GATE LEAVES A WITNESS, because until now NOTHING RECORDED THAT IT RAN. Measured: the
# only trace of a pre-commit run was its stdout, which is gone the moment the terminal scrolls.
# So `did the gate run for this commit` was UNANSWERABLE — and a policy of *never `--no-verify`*
# that cannot be checked after the fact is a policy that reads as enforced and is not.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE WITNESS KEYS ON THE STAGED TREE, NOT ON HEAD, and that is forced rather than chosen: this
# hook runs BEFORE the commit object exists, so there is no SHA to name yet. `write-tree` is the
# tree the gate actually verified, and the commit that follows carries that same tree — so a later
# reader recomputes the key from `git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}` and compares. A commit made with
# `--no-verify` produces a tree this file never names.
#
# ⚑ IT IS A WITNESS, NOT A LOCK. It proves the gate ran over these bytes; it cannot prove nobody
# edited afterward, and it is deliberately not in the repo (`.git/` is not committed) because a
# committed witness would be a claim about a run that the next clone did not perform.
_witness_tree=$(git write-tree 2>/dev/null || true)
if [ -n "${_witness_tree:-}" ]; then
    mkdir -p "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/mtools"
    printf '%s\n' "$_witness_tree" \
        >> "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/mtools/gate-verified"
fi
say "ok"
exit 0
